TITLE="NBIS Bear Case Outline (Red Team), v3.3: ranked by impact"
META_LINE="Nebius Skeptic for Aadit Prayag · draft for Tue Oct 6, 2026 · prices as of close Fri Oct 2, 2026 · v3.3 compiled Sun Oct 4, 2026, ~7:20 PM ET (backups: 02_bear_outline_v3.2.* / _v3.1.* / _v2.* / _v1.*)"
TAGS="Tags: R = Reported · D = Derived (math shown) · E = Estimated (method stated). Probabilities are Red Team judgment, not data. The $/share figures use EV $74.73B, 308.69M economic basic shares (271.86M issued + 15.77M Aug exchange + 21.07M NVIDIA pre-funded warrant; Filings CapStructure, reversal of Oct 4) and $242.81. Fully diluted is 381.5M."
SETUP=[
 "Hurdle (D): EV $74.73B = 6.08x FY27 consensus revenue ($12.30B) and 11.1x FY27 adj. EBITDA ($6.73B). CoreWeave on the same ex-lease basis is 3.03x / 4.78x, so NBIS carries a ~+101% / ~+132% premium. At CRWV's EBITDA multiple, the stock would be ~$105/share (−57%). At CRWV's revenue multiple, ~$121 (−50%).",
 "Rule of thumb (D): at a constant 6.08x, each $1B of FY27 revenue is worth ~$6.1B of EV, or ~$19.7/share.",
 "Reverse DCF (E; "+D03+" Reverse_DCF): the price requires a FY29+ EBITDA yield on gross BILLING GPU capital of ~48% at a 5-yr life (~52% at 4 yrs, ~45% at 6 yrs). On the same basis, FY28 consensus gives ~31% (27% on the v1 in-service basis). At 31%, the DCF EV is negative under every life (−$83B / −$52B / −$31B at 4/5/6 yrs). That holds even if FY29–30 capex halves each year (EV −$48B / −$23B / −$6B; implied yield 51/47/44%). FY28 consensus equals ~$11.0M of revenue and ~$6.7M of EBITDA per billing gross MW on Capacity's base MW path.",
 "Cohort unit economics (E; "+D03+" Cohorts; per gross MW; Capacity capex; 11% WACC). Project IRR at a 5-yr life, owned / leased: installed book ($12M, Hopper-era capex) 15% / 12%. Q2 deals $20M 22% / 23%. Q2 deals $25M 32% / 38%. Short-term >$40M (1-yr, then re-rent on A100-evidence residuals) 22% / 24%. Microsoft at $11.6M/gross MW (300 MW basis) −5% (leased), and 15% if the contract covers only 200 MW. Prepayments (55% of capex) lift equity IRRs to ~25–75% for the non-Microsoft cohorts. So the economics depend on new-deal pricing, not on the installed book or on Microsoft.",
 "Site model correction: Nebius is mostly leased, like CoreWeave (started leases $1,625M; $12.05B signed but not commenced). v1's 'owns more sites' framing is dropped. Leased sites need less capex (no shell) but pay rent of ~$2.0–2.5M per IT MW-year. At the Q2-deal ACV, leased-site project IRRs come out at or above owned-site IRRs.",
]
LEGS=[
 dict(t="Site execution / billing-MW ramp: FY27 consensus needs Capacity's HIGH case",
  claim="On Capacity's named-site quarterly energized MW (Quarterly MW tab: 185/305/424 MW at YE26 and 764/1,069/1,374 at YE27, low/base/high), shifted one quarter for connected-to-billing, FY27 revenue reaches $12.3B only in the high case. The guide is 800 MW–1 GW connected at YE26, last restated orally on the Aug 12 call.",
  ev=["Capacity energized MW by site and quarter (E, "+CAP+"). Sites without a stated basis are treated as gross. Vineland and AIB are IT MW (×1.25 to gross)",
      "Filings: the 'few months' lag runs from connected power to revenue. The model uses 1 quarter by default and tests 2 (R/E, "+FX+")",
      "Q2'26 calibration: ~134 MW non-Vineland billing × $12M/4 + Microsoft T1–T2 ≈ $595M vs $582.3M reported (D)",
      "Pricing (R, Q2 letter "+Q2L+"): installed book at $12M per gross MW-yr. Incremental MW at the $20–25M Q2-deal midpoint ($22.5M). Oct 1 hike applied to the ~4% on-demand + Token Factory share. Microsoft from the tranche schedule (leg 2)"],
  q="FY27 revenue (E, "+D03+" Rev_MW; gap vs $12.3B; $/share at 6.08x): base $9.94B (−$2.36B, −$46). Low $7.01B (−$5.29B, −$104). High $12.86B (+$0.56B, +$11). Base with a 2-qtr lag $7.62B (−$92). Low + 2-qtr lag + Microsoft bear $5.16B (−$141). Unseen sites (Oklahoma and Minnesota are candidates): closing the base gap needs ~42 gross MW added every quarter from Q4'26 (~$56.7M of FY27 revenue per 1 MW/qtr). FY28 bottom-up ($25.3B base) is above consensus ($23.0B), so this is mostly a timing bear: 2027, not the end state.",
  p="25% low / 50% base / 25% high (1-qtr lag)",
  e="≈ −$46/share (E: 0.25×−104 + 0.5×−46 + 0.25×+11)",
  bull="Management has beaten capacity goals before (contracted 5 GW vs >4 GW). Undisclosed colo sites (Oklahoma, Minnesota, France 2nd, UK 3rd) could plug the gap, and short-term deals at >$40M/MW monetize whatever comes online early.",
  watch="Q3 letter connected/active MW (~Nov 10); Capacity's billing-MW row (Tue); energization events at Vineland (Bloom), Béthune, Independence, Birmingham and Kansas City."),
 dict(t="Microsoft / Vineland: a physical genset ceiling turns slippage into contract-value risk",
  claim="Vineland is a leased build-to-suit site running on 62 rented gas generators (1.98 MW each, 122.9 MW gross ceiling) and ~5 MW of grid power. The Bloom fuel cells and LNG tank are under stop-construction orders (Aug 6/10). Until Bloom is live, only ~4 of 9 tranches fit (~27 MW IT each, equal-size assumption), which caps the book near ~$1.4–1.5B/yr (~$350–390M/qtr) vs ~$3.5B/yr fully delivered. Microsoft can cancel a tranche after a missed delivery date plus grace period if no alternative capacity is offered, and late delivery triggers liquidated damages.",
  ev=["Contract (R, 6-K Sep 8 2025 "+MS6K+"; 20-F Note 1): 9 tranches, $17.39B owed regardless of use (take-or-pay), ~$6.96B upfront, billed through Oct-2031",
      "Physical evidence (E, Capacity): est. live IT load ~33–45 MW at end-Q2 and ~57–75 MW in Q3. The NJ regulator's 45-day permit deadline falls in early-to-mid Nov",
      "Capacity tranche track: T1–T4 within the genset ceiling. T5–T9 bill Q2–Q3'27 (base) or Q3'27–Q1'28 (bear)",
      "CONFLICT: the Aug 12 letter says all tranches are delivered, yet the Q2 top customer was ~$140M/qtr (24% × $582.3M) vs ~$193M for T1+T2 at an equal split and ~$870M fully delivered"],
  q="(E, "+D03+" Microsoft; equal split $96.6M/qtr per tranche; haircut = SLA/service credits + per-tranche termination, not demand). FY27 Microsoft revenue: 'all delivered' $3.48B · base $2.71B · bear $2.13B · T5–T9 cancelled $1.55B. FY27 total revenue vs base: bear −$0.58B (−$11/share); T5–T9 cancelled −$1.16B (−$23/share; −$69 vs consensus). PV of Microsoft revenue at 11%: $13.4B / $12.3B / $11.8B / $5.8B. Unit economics (Cohorts): at $11.6M/gross MW on GB300 leased capex (~$33M per gross MW), project IRR is ~−5% even at a 5-yr life. So the contract only works if it covers ~200 MW ($17.4M/MW, IRR 15%) or if Microsoft-related capex is far lower than Capacity's base.",
  p="35% bear timing (Bloom permit slips into 2027); 10% that T5–T9 are cancelled or re-cut",
  e="≈ −$6/share incremental to leg 1 (E: 0.35×−11 + 0.10×−23); tail −$23 to −$69",
  bull="Take-or-pay once delivered. Nebius can offer alternative capacity to avoid cancellation. The grace periods on IREN's similar Microsoft contract ran into 2027. The ~$6.96B upfront has already funded the build.",
  watch="NJ permit decision (early–mid Nov); Q3 top-customer % and deferred-revenue release; any 6-K on Vineland power; claim 1 below."),
 dict(t="Unit economics / ROIC: the price needs ~48% yield on billing capital vs ~31% in FY28 consensus",
  claim="The equity case needs Q2-deal economics ($20–25M/MW) to hold across the whole fleet for the asset life. FY28 consensus itself implies only ~$11M of revenue per billing MW on Capacity's MW path, and the installed book and the Microsoft contract earn far less than new deals.",
  ev=["ACV/MW: installed ~$12M; Q2 deals $20–25M, priced on forecast costs and including capacity not yet built; Q3 short-term >$40M (R, "+Q2L+")",
      "Billing-capital basis (Modeling pushback accepted): FY28 EBITDA $13.98B / avg billing GPU capital = 31.0% vs 27.2% in service (D, "+D03+" Model)",
      "Owned vs leased fleet: FY28 yield 33% if all-owned vs 29% if all-leased; market-implied 49% vs 41% at 5 yrs (E)",
      "Prepayments: 70% of deals cover 50–60% of capex. That helps equity returns (not project returns) and is repaid through lower future cash revenue (R, "+FB+")"],
  q="(E, "+D03+" Reverse_DCF) Value gap at the FY28 billing yield: −$510 / −$410 / −$343 per share (4/5/6 yrs). The 48% (5-yr) breakeven compares with 2026 actuals of ~41% EBITDA margin on the installed book.",
  p="35% that the realized fleet yield settles at ≤40% on billing capital",
  e="≈ −$65/share (E). This overlaps with legs 1 and 7 (same yield equation), so they are not additive.",
  bull="Q2/Q3 pricing is far above the installed book. Equity IRRs on prepaid deals run ~40–75%. Consensus FY28 revenue is below Capacity's base-case bottom-up ($25.3B).",
  watch="Q3 ACV/MW on new deals; active-MW disclosure; prepayment inflows vs the >$9B 2026 guide."),
 dict(t="Governance / controls & earnings quality (raised; claim 4 CONFIRMED)",
  claim="Both judgments that set D&A are weak. Depreciation start dates sit on failed controls, and the life change came right after year-end in the company's favor. Separately, the Aug 12 letter said all Microsoft tranches were delivered while Vineland ran on unpermitted temporary power, and no 6-K discloses that.",
  ev=["CONFIRMED (Filings, high confidence; "+FX+" Controls_Depreciation): FY25 20-F Item 15 p.85 says ICFR is not effective. The weakness covers depreciation start dates and asset count. Second year running, with an adverse ICFR opinion from Reanda; remediation pushed to end-2026 (R, "+F20+")",
      "Earnings-quality dollars (R): the life change adds ~$43M a quarter ($167.6M FY26 guided). Capitalized interest was $15.7M in Q1 and $40.2M in Q2, vs $107.5M of gross convert interest in Q2. Together that is ~$83M of Q2 pre-tax benefit, vs $236.2M of adj. EBITDA",
      "EBIT target: the 20–30% medium-term margin was framed in May 2025 'assuming a conservative depreciation schedule of four years', then reaffirmed on Feb 12 2026 without that caveat, on the same call that moved servers to 5 yrs (R, "+FX+" Guidance_Log). On a 4-yr basis, FY27/FY28 consensus gives 7.4%/5.6% EBIT. The life change adds ~9.6/10.5 pts and still leaves 17.0%/16.1%, below the target (D)",
      "Depreciation starting late: downgraded to untested-but-not-supported. In-service transfers were $1.78B in Q1 and $0.76B in Q2, with $7.84B not yet in use at 6/30. D&A / avg in-service PP&E ex-life-change is 21.6% (Q1) and 20.0% (Q2), consistent with 5-yr straight-line (Filings, Note 7)",
      "Other: the CEO family trust holds Class B with ~52% of votes and ~11% of economics; the auditor moves from Reanda to Deloitte for FY26; NVIDIA is supplier and investor (21.07M pre-funded warrant). Andrey Korolenko is Chief Product & Infrastructure Officer (not CFO)"],
  q="(E) A 10–15% de-rating on a restatement, a delayed 20-F or a tranche-disclosure issue ≈ −$24 to −$36/share.",
  p="15%",
  e="≈ −$4/share (E)",
  bull="No misstatement has been found. The auditor did not make the life change a critical audit matter (the PP&E CAM covers placed-in-use dates and capitalization). Moving to a Big-4 auditor is a positive.",
  watch="Forward test: does Q3'26 show a large transfer to in-service after the Jul–Aug Vineland tranches? Q3 D&A / in-service ex-change; any 6-K on Vineland power; FY26 20-F ICFR opinion."),
 dict(t="Dilution / refinancing: base-case funding gap opens in Q4'27 (path-consistent capex)",
  claim="H2'26 is funded. v3.2 base case: capex follows Capacity's energized-MW path (owned and leased capex per IT MW by generation), not consensus. On that basis liquidity first goes negative in Q4'27 and the gap is ~$7B by YE28. Consensus capex ($29.1B FY27 / $38.9B FY28) buys MW faster than Capacity sees them energizing, and on that basis the gap opens in Q2'27. Either the funding gap or the revenue gap (leg 1) shows up.",
  ev=["Starting liquidity $14,565M = 6/30 unrestricted cash $8.04B + Aug converts $5.75B + secured loan up to $0.775B. Prepayments are seeded from $5.98B of deferred revenue, and only new prepayments count as inflows (R/D, Filings)",
      "Path-consistent capex (E, "+D03+" Model; Capacity Quarterly MW). Owned: GB200 ~$42M / GB300 ~$53M / VR ~$43M per IT MW incl. ~$10–12.5M shell, spent 2 qtrs before energization. Leased, IT only: GB200 ~$30M / GB300 ~$41M / VR ~$32M per IT MW (Estimated, medium confidence, Capacity) plus rent of $2.0–2.5M per MW-yr. GPUs bought 1 qtr before billing",
      "Zero-liquidity quarter / YE27 / YE28 (E). Path low: none by 2030 / +$7.1B / +$1.5B. PATH BASE: Q4'27 / −$0.15B / −$6.8B. Path high: Q3'27 / −$7.4B / −$15.6B. Consensus capex: Q2'27 / −$9.9B / −$27.7B. Expected YE27 ~−$1.5B / YE28 ~−$8.8B holds only if VR leased capex is priced like GB300 ($41M): −$1.45B / −$8.7B",
      "Caveat: EBITDA is held at consensus on the path basis, which flatters liquidity (Capacity's base path supports only ~$9.9B of FY27 revenue)",
      "Converts $13.43B, net-share settlement default, NO capped calls. Calls possible above 130% of conversion; the first is Dec 2026 for the leftover 2029s (R, Filings CapStructure)",
      "FD at $242.81: 381.5M (RSUs 6.23M, options 4.28M on TSM, ITM converts 50.02M, ATM 12.27M). All converts regardless of price: 399.6M; net-share settlement 349.9M, needing ~$7.7B of cash (R/D)"],
  q="(D) At constant EV, crediting converted principal (~$7.68B ITM) and ATM proceeds (~$2.98B): ~$224/share on 381.5M (−$18, −8%). Funding the base-case YE28 gap (~$6.8B) with equity at a 20% discount ($194, ~35M shares) costs another ~$3/share. On consensus capex (YE28 −$27.7B, ~143M shares) it costs ~−$8/share. Dilution steps down as the price falls through $183 (2031/2033 tranches), $139 (2030/2032) and $51 (leftover 2029/2031).",
  p="70%",
  e="≈ −$15/share (E: 0.7 × (−18 −3))",
  bull="Prepayments (>$9B guided for 2026) and secured GPU financing reduce equity needs. NBIS's cost of debt is 5.1% vs CRWV's 8.4%. On Capacity's low path there is no gap through 2030.",
  watch="Q3 cash and debt; ATM usage; any new convert or ABS in 2H27; Capacity's billing-MW row (Tue) to re-time the path."),
 dict(t="Meta #2 timing: no named site energizes in 1H27",
  claim="Meta #2 ($12B firm on Vera Rubin, plus up to $15B backstop likely outside RPO) has tranches due 'throughout 1H27'. MAIN risk is site readiness: no named site energizes in 1H27 (Independence Q3'27, Birmingham 230kV Oct'27, Béthune slipping). SECONDARY risk is Vera Rubin timing: Nebius has only validated a first Vera Rubin rack (Jul 23) vs CoreWeave GA Sep 30. GPU Market's 2–6 month lag behind CoreWeave implies Nebius Rubin GA around Dec'26–Mar'27, still inside 1H27.",
  ev=["Meta #2 terms (R, "+META+")","Site dates (E, "+CAP+")","Rubin validation (R, Nebius Jul 23) vs CRWV GA Sep 30 (R); lag estimate (E, GPU Market)"],
  q="(E) At ~$2.4B/yr when fully delivered, each quarter of slip removes ~$0.6B of run-rate revenue (~$12/share at 6.08x if it falls in FY27). Two quarters ≈ −$24/share. This overlaps with leg 1, since the slip shows up in the MW path.",
  p="50% that ≥1 quarter slips",
  e="≈ −$8/share (E; largely inside leg 1)",
  bull="Meta could take capacity at colo/unseen sites. Termination needs a missed date plus grace period. Rubin GA timing is still inside 1H27.",
  watch="Independence/Birmingham energization; Nebius Rubin GA announcement; Q3 RPO change."),
 dict(t="Price cycle + earnings quality (re-ranked lower: a short economic life is NOT supported)",
  claim="v3.1 restatement: GPU Market's A100 evidence supports a 5–6 yr economic life, so the depreciation bear no longer rests on a short life. It rests on (i) a Blackwell price cycle after Rubin launches and (ii) earnings quality: the life change and capitalized interest flatter EBIT.",
  ev=["A100 (launched Nov-20) still rents near list in year 6. Hyperscaler list fell only −1.8%/yr 2020–26. The neocloud composite ROSE +12%/yr ($1.23 → $2.00). The 80GB median went $1.87 → $2.50. Spot median is roughly flat. These are list prices (R, /workspace/nebius/market/gpu_price_history.xlsx A100_Decay)",
      "Residual index in "+D03+" now uses −1.8%/yr (0.91x at age 6) × 60% re-rent utilization. 6-yr vs 5-yr project IRR gains only ~1.5–3 pts per cohort, so life is not the swing factor (E)",
      "Earnings quality (R, "+FX+" Controls_Depreciation): the life change adds ~$43M/qtr ($167.6M FY26 guided; ~14% of Q2 D&A). Capitalized interest was $15.7M Q1 and $40.2M Q2 vs $107.5M of gross convert interest. On a 4-yr basis, FY27/FY28 consensus EBIT margin is 7.4%/5.6% vs 17.0%/16.1% at 5 yrs (+9.6/+10.5 pts)",
      "Revenue mix (GPU Market): take-or-pay 45% (not exposed), reserved ~50% (exposed as contracts renew), on-demand + Token Factory ~4% (fully exposed). The Oct 1 list hike (+17–21%) touches only ~4% of revenue (~+0.8%)"],
  q="Price cycle (E, "+D03+" Rev_MW): a 27.5% (25–30%) Blackwell $/GPU-hr cut phased in over the 12 months after Rubin launches (Q1'27), on exposed revenue only (on-demand fully; reserved only as contracts renew), with the 5-yr life unchanged. Reserved-book renewal scenarios agreed with Modeling, FY27 revenue = EBITDA hit: BULL (Modeling's original schedule) −$75M; BASE (50% of the reserved book renews within FY27) −$340M; BEAR (100% renews within FY27) −$782M. The scenarios come from Nebius pricing-page terms, not a filing; Modeling's earlier schedule was a model assumption. Basis note: base and bull are bottom-up; the bear −$782M is scaled to consensus (−$632M bottom-up). On a consistent consensus-scaled basis the base is −$420M (−6.2% of FY27 EBITDA, ≈ −$15/share at 11.1x). FY28 −$1.6B (−$1.0B to −$2.2B by MW case) ≈ −$28/share at 5.35x.",
  p="40% that a Blackwell price cycle of this size materializes after Rubin GA",
  e="≈ −$11/share (E: 0.4 × FY28 −$28). Overlaps with leg 3 (yield)",
  bull="A100 history argues for long economic lives and resilient list prices. Supply is tight. RPO of $37.5B (36% within 24 months) and the 45% take-or-pay share lock in near-term pricing. No misstatement has been found, and the life change is not a CAM.",
  watch="Reserved-book renewal timing vs page terms (bull/base/bear); Blackwell list and spot prices after Rubin GA (Dec'26–Mar'27); Nebius commit-discount wording; renewal pricing in the Q4'26 and Q1'27 letters; Q3 6-K D&A / in-service ratio and capitalized interest.")
]
